1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,462
What Causes the Seasons?

2
00:00:02,412 --> 00:00:07,987
A widespread misconception holds that summer occurs when Earth approaches closest to the Sun.

3
00:00:07,987 --> 00:00:14,350
The actual cause is Earth's axial tilt of 23.44 degrees relative to its orbital plane.

4
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:24,012
As Earth orbits the Sun over 365.25 days, the tilt orientation remains fixed in space, pointing toward Polaris.

5
00:00:24,012 --> 00:00:36,725
During the June solstice, the Northern Hemisphere is inclined toward the Sun, receiving sunlight at a steeper angle (higher solar elevation) for longer daylight hours — up to 24 hours above the Arctic Circle.

6
00:00:36,725 --> 00:00:41,037
The concentrated solar flux per unit area results in summer.

7
00:00:41,037 --> 00:00:49,887
Simultaneously, the Southern Hemisphere experiences winter because sunlight arrives at a shallow angle, spreading the same energy over a larger area.

8
00:00:49,887 --> 00:00:53,450
The situation reverses at the December solstice.

9
00:00:53,450 --> 00:00:59,400
Earth's orbit is slightly elliptical, with an eccentricity of 0.0167.

10
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:14,725
At perihelion in early January, Earth is approximately 147.1 million kilometers from the Sun; at aphelion in early July, it is about 152.1 million kilometers — a variation of roughly 5 million kilometers.

11
00:01:14,725 --> 00:01:23,175
This distance change alters total solar irradiance by only about 6%, insufficient to cause the seasonal temperature contrasts observed.

12
00:01:23,175 --> 00:01:29,950
The axial tilt produces a much larger variation of up to 100% in insolation at mid-latitudes.

13
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:38,462
Seasonal lag — the delay of approximately one month between solstices and peak temperatures — results from thermal inertia of land and oceans.

14
00:01:38,462 --> 00:01:50,162
Milankovitch cycles, which describe long-term variations in Earth's orbit and tilt over tens of thousands of years, explain the timing of ice ages via changes in seasonal insolation distribution.

